#### Discriminative Scaling

The scaling patterns on the mortgage dataset are broadly consistent with the credit-card findings but with important differences driven by the low base rate.

**SVGP dominates at small scale.** With only 425 training observations, SVGP achieves ROC-AUC = 0.764 — the highest of any model at any small-scale setting across both datasets. This is a 0.064 advantage over random forest and DSVGP-2L (both 0.692) and a 0.044 advantage over logistic regression (0.720). The single-layer GP's inductive bias through the kernel function and the variational framework's implicit regularisation provide a strong prior for the smooth risk surface of the mortgage domain, even with extremely limited training data.

**DGP architectures are data-hungry.** DSVGP-2L achieves only 0.692 at small scale — the same as random forest — then surges to 0.815 at medium scale and 0.821 at large scale, a gain of +0.129. This is the largest absolute ROC-AUC improvement of any model across tiers on either dataset, confirming that the compositional flexibility of multi-layer GP architectures requires sufficient data to train the inter-layer mappings effectively. DSVGP-3L again collapses at small scale (0.510, near-random) and recovers to 0.813/0.823 at medium/large — the same pathological behaviour observed on the credit-card dataset and consistent with @duvenaud_avoiding_2014.

**Classical scaling.** Logistic regression shows the strongest classical scaling response: ROC-AUC = 0.720 (small) to 0.820 (large), a gain of +0.100. This is larger than its credit-card gain (+0.023), suggesting that the mortgage feature space contains exploitable monotonic structure that benefits from more data. XGBoost improves by +0.085 (0.700 → 0.785), while random forest gains +0.082 (0.692 → 0.774). Both tree ensembles underperform the GP family at every scale on this dataset.

#### Calibration Scaling

**GP models achieve near-perfect calibration from 425 observations.** SVGP's ECE is 0.008 at small scale, 0.001 at medium, and 0.003 at large. DSVGP-2L follows a similar trajectory (0.006 → 0.002 → 0.002). The medium-tier ECE values of 0.001–0.002 represent calibration indistinguishable from perfect at this sample size. This is a remarkable finding: even with only 425 training examples drawn from a 1.73%-prevalence population (approximately 7–8 positive examples), the Bayesian posterior's uncertainty propagation produces well-calibrated probability estimates.

**Classical calibration is unstable and non-monotonic.** Logistic regression's ECE *worsens* from 0.118 (small) to 0.328 (medium) to 0.337 (large). XGBoost exhibits even more erratic calibration (0.319 → 0.180 → 0.400). This non-monotonic behaviour arises because, as with the credit card dataset, additional training data allows classical models to fit more extreme score distributions without an intrinsic mechanism to constrain probabilities toward calibrated values [@niculescu_mizil_predicting_2005]. Random forest remains the best-calibrated classical model (ECE = 0.044–0.055 across tiers) due to the same bagging-induced smoothing effect described in @sec-uci-large-calibration, but it is still 10–25$\times$ worse than the GP family.

### Computational Profile {#sec-fm-cost}

![Training time versus ECE for the six models at large scale. GP-family models occupy the lower-right quadrant: higher training cost but dramatically better calibration.](/sections/4-modeling/assets/freddie-mac/large/runtime.png){#fig-fm-runtime width=70%}

| Model | Time (s, small) | Time (s, medium) | Time (s, large) |
|:------|:-------:|:-------:|:-------:|
| Logistic Regression | 0.08 | 0.01 | 0.06 |
| Random Forest | 0.45 | 0.81 | 2.36 |
| XGBoost | 0.03 | 0.10 | 0.20 |
| SVGP | 3.53 | 33.4 | 148.7 |
| DSVGP-2L | 3.59 | 37.1 | 167.8 |
| DSVGP-3L | 12.9 | 136.5 | 650.2 |

: Training time scaling across tiers. Scaling patterns are consistent with the credit-card dataset; DSVGP-3L is approximately $3.9\times$ the cost of DSVGP-2L. {#tbl-fm-runtime}

The computational profile mirrors the credit-card findings. DSVGP-3L at 650.2 s is the most expensive model, approximately $3.9\times$ the cost of DSVGP-2L (167.8 s), yet its ROC-AUC advantage over DSVGP-2L is only +0.002 (0.823 vs 0.821) and its ECE is slightly worse (0.004 vs 0.002). SVGP provides the best cost-effectiveness among GP models: at 148.7 s it delivers ROC-AUC = 0.819 and ECE = 0.003, within 0.004 ROC-AUC of the best model at less than one-quarter the training cost of DSVGP-3L.

### Discussion {#sec-fm-discussion}

**Discriminative performance.** The mortgage dataset produces a different competitive landscape from the credit-card benchmark. GP and DGP models lead the field on ROC-AUC rather than trailing XGBoost: DSVGP-3L (0.823) > DSVGP-2L (0.821) > LR (0.820) > SVGP (0.819) $\gg$ XGB (0.785) > RF (0.774). The GP family's advantage is clearer when the feature space is structured and the risk surface is smooth, consistent with the theoretical expectation that kernel-based models excel when the underlying function belongs to the reproducing kernel Hilbert space induced by the chosen kernel [@rasmussen_gaussian_2008]. The tree-based models' relative weakness on this dataset likely reflects their difficulty in modelling the smooth, approximately monotonic relationships between FICO/LTV/DTI and default probability — they approximate smooth functions through piecewise-constant partitions, introducing unnecessary granularity that reduces ranking quality relative to the GP's continuous representation.

**Probability calibration.** The mortgage dataset provides the strongest evidence for the native calibration advantage of GP and DGP models. At a 1.73% base rate, GP models achieve ECE = 0.002–0.004 — essentially perfect calibration — while classical models produce ECE = 0.055–0.400. This 100$\times$ calibration advantage is directly transferable to regulatory capital adequacy: GP-produced PD estimates can be used without adjustment in Basel IRB capital formulae [@basel_committee_basel_2011], IFRS 9 expected credit loss calculations [@iasb_ifrs9_2014], and stress-testing models, whereas classical models require a post-hoc calibration stage. In practice, the calibration stage introduces both development cost and an additional validation burden under SR 11-7 model risk management requirements [@federal_reserve_sr11_7], making the GP's native calibration a material operational simplification.

**Architecture depth.** On the mortgage dataset, the three-layer DGP achieves a modest ROC-AUC improvement over the two-layer variant (+0.002) for the first time across both datasets — a tentative signal that the richer risk driver interactions in mortgage data may benefit from deeper compositional structure. However, the improvement is small relative to the $3.9\times$ computational cost increase (650 s vs 168 s), and DSVGP-3L's ECE (0.004) is slightly worse than DSVGP-2L's (0.002). At small scale, DSVGP-3L again collapses (ROC-AUC = 0.510), reproducing the @duvenaud_avoiding_2014 pathology seen on the credit-card data. The evidence does not support using three-layer architectures for mortgage PD estimation.

**GP vs DGP.** The single-layer SVGP provides an excellent cost-calibration trade-off on this dataset. At large scale, its ROC-AUC (0.819) is within 0.004 of the best DGP (DSVGP-3L), and its ECE (0.003) is near-indistinguishable from DSVGP-2L's (0.002). At small scale, SVGP dramatically outperforms both DGP architectures (0.764 vs 0.692/0.510). The additional compositional flexibility of the DGP primarily manifests at medium-to-large scale, where DSVGP-2L's +0.003 ROC-AUC advantage over SVGP at large scale, and +0.003 at medium scale, represent marginal gains. For mortgage PD applications where model simplicity and small-sample robustness are valued — as is typically the case for low-default portfolios — the single-layer SVGP is the more defensible choice.

**Cross-dataset comparison.** Comparing the mortgage and credit-card results reveals a consistent pattern: GP models are competitive or superior on discrimination and dramatically superior on calibration in both settings, but the magnitude of the advantage is amplified on the low-default mortgage dataset. The calibration advantage grows from 12–13$\times$ (credit card, 22.1% default rate) to 100–170$\times$ (mortgage, 1.73% default rate), consistent with the Bayesian interpretation that the posterior's uncertainty quantification becomes more valuable as the prediction target moves further from 0.5 and classical models' implicit assumptions about score-to-probability mappings break down more severely [@niculescu_mizil_predicting_2005]. This finding carries a direct practical implication: the lower the default rate in a portfolio, the greater the relative advantage of GP models for producing regulatory-grade PD estimates.
